<section xml:id="section_3-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Sampling from a finite population</title>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-literary-computing">
  <title>Literary Computing</title>

  <figure width="100%" xml:id="gettysburg-1">
    <image source="3-1/gettysburg_1.png">
      <description>The text of Lincoln's Gettysburg address, which begins: Fourscore and seven years ago… and ends: shall not perish from the earth.
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 1-->
  <statement>
    <p>
      Select a representative set of 10 words from the passage above by circling them with your pen
  or pencil.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Answers will vary. One option follows: score, engaged, larger, living, devotion, freedom,
      government, people, world, not
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<p>The authorship of literary works is often a topic for debate. For example, researchers have
tried to determine whether some of the works attributed to Shakespeare were actually written by Bacon or Marlow. The field of “literary computing” examines ways of numerically analyzing authors' works, looking at variables such as sentence length and rates of occurrence of specific words.
</p>
<activity> 
 <!--Question 2-->
 <statement>
  <p>
    Complete the following data table by recording each word from your sample and then
    indicate the length of the word (number of letters) and whether or not the word is "short" (3 or fewer letters).
  </p>
  <image source="3-1/word_length_1.png">
    <description> A table is shown. There are 10 rows, with columns labeled &amp;qout;Word&amp;qout;, &amp;qout;Length (no. of letters)&amp;qout;, and &amp;qout;Short (3 or fewer letters)? (Y or N)&amp;qout;
    </description>
  </image>
 </statement>
 <solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary.  Double check that they categorized their words correctly.
  </p>
 </solution>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 3-->
<statement>
<p>Identify the observational units and the variables you have recorded on these
observational units (Keep in mind that observational units do not have to be people!)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Observational units = each word, variables = length (number of letters), short or not
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<definition>
 <statement><p>The <term>population</term> is the entire collection of observational units of interest, and the
<term>sample</term> is a subset of the population on which we record data.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The passage is, of course, Lincoln's Gettysburg Address, given November 19, 1863, on the
battlefield near Gettysburg, Pennsylvania. We are considering this passage a <term>population</term> of
268 words, and the 10 words you selected are a <term>sample</term> from this population. As before, we can
use <m>\hat{p}</m> to refer to a sample proportion and <m>\pi </m> to refer to the parameter, this time the proportion
of short words in the population.
</p>
<image source="3-1/gettysburg_2.png" width="50%">
  <description>The Gettysburg Address of Abraham Lincoln is shown.</description>
</image>

<activity>
 <!--Question 4-->
<statement>
<p>Do you think the sample proportion of short words you found will be a good estimate of
the population proportion, <m>\pi= 0.41 </m> ? Why or why not?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary. Most will say yes, because they feel the words they selected are
    representative of all the words in the address.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 5-->
<statement>
<p>Suggest a method for deciding whether the sample proportion of short words is likely
to be close to the population proportion of short words. (Hint: Whereas any one sample
may not produce a statistic that exactly equals the population parameter, what would we
like to be true in general?)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Select many samples of 10 words and calculate the proportion of short words for each sample.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="q-3-2-6"> 
 <!--Question 6-->
 <statement>
  <p>
    Combine your results with your classmates' by producing a dotplot of the distribution of
    the proportion of short words in your samples.
  </p>
 </statement>
 <solution>
  <p>
    Dotplot of sample proportions is produced in class. An example from a class of 25 students follows.
  </p>
  <image source="3-1/exercise-6.png">
    <shortdescription>An example dotplot</shortdescription>
  </image>
 </solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 7-->
<statement>
<p>What label should be along the horizontal axis of this graph? How many dots do you
have? In other words, clarify what each dot represents. (Hint: If you wanted to add
another dot to the graph, what would you need to do?)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Horizontal axis has label of “Proportion of short words.” There are 25 dots. Each dot represents
    the proportion of short words from a sample of 10 words. To produce another dot, one must
    take another sample of 10 words.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 8-->
<statement>
<p>Based on the class dotplot, does this sampling method tend to produce sample
proportions close to the population proportion, 0.41? Justify your answer.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    No, all the sample proportions of short words are less than 0.41.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<definition>
 <statement>
  <p>A sampling method is <term>biased</term> if, when using that sampling method, statistics
from different samples <em>consistently</em> overestimate or consistently underestimate the
population parameter of interest.</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<p>Note that bias is a property of a sampling <em>method</em>, not a property of an individual sample.
Also note that the sampling method must <em>consistently</em> produce nonrepresentative results in
the same direction in order to be considered biased</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 9-->
<statement>
<p>Does asking you to quickly select 10 representative words appear to be a biased or
unbiased sampling method? If biased, what is the direction of the bias (tendency to
overestimate or underestimate the proportion of short words)?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Biased, this sampling method consistently underestimates the proportion of short words.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 10-->
<statement>
<p>Explain why we might have expected this sampling method (asking you to quickly pick
10 representative words) to be biased for this variable.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Our eyes tend to be drawn to larger words and so the shorter words were under-sampled.
    Individuals may also look for words that convey more “meaning” of the passage which will tend
    to be longer.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 11-->
<statement>
<p>Do you think that if we'd asked each of you to take 20 words instead of 10 words it
would have helped with this issue? Explain.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    No, the method is still the same (biased), so the statistics will continue to under-estimate the
    parameter.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 12-->
<statement>
<p>Suggest another technique for selecting 10 words from this population in order for the
sampling method to be unbiased and likely to produce samples that are representative
of the population proportion of short words.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers may vary. Eg.: Write each word on the same size slip of paper and place slips in a hat.
    Mix slips of paper and draw out 10 of them.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>
</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Taking a Simple Random Sample</title>
<remark> 
<p>A <term>simple random</term> sample ensures that every sample of size <em>n</em> is equally likely to be
the sample selected from the population. In particular, each observational unit has the
same chance of being selected as every other observational unit.</p>
</remark>

<p>The key to obtaining a representative sample is using some type of <em> random</em> mechanism to
select the observational units from the population, rather than relying on <term>convenience samples</term>
or any type of human judgment.</p>

<definition>
 <statement><p>A <term>convenience sample</term> is a nonrandom sample of a population.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Instead of having you choose “random” words using your own judgment, we will now ask
you to take a simple random sample of words and evaluate your results. The first step is to obtain
a <term>sampling frame</term>—a complete list of every member of the population where each member of
the population can be assigned a number. Below is a copy of the Gettysburg Address that includes
numbers in front of every word. For example, the 43rd word is <q>nation</q>. <em>Note</em>: In this sampling
frame, “four score” is listed as two words.</p>

<activity> 
